UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 7, Week 17, Lesson 3: Early modern words and meanings', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Early Modern words may look familiar but shift meaning (false friends). Use glosses/footnotes, compare word order and build a personal glossary. Do not guess sacred or historical claims beyond the text/class notes.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same strategy to an unfamiliar topic or audience, then compare the effect of two different choices.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nGloss two unfamiliar words from your scene and modernise one inverted sentence.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: Accurate glosses; modernisation preserves sense.\n\nCHECK\nSkipping glosses and bluffing. Precision beats guessing.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nBuild a 12-word glossary from your play excerpt with modern meaning and a sentence using each modern sense.\n\nWrite a short reflection describing one specific revision you made and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"English","learning_intention":"Early Modern words may look familiar but shift meaning (false friends). Use glosses/footnotes, compare word order and build a personal glossary. Do not guess sacred or historical claims beyond the text/class notes.","lesson_number":3,"lesson_role":"Language and vocabulary","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["EN4-ECB-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Build a 12-word glossary from your play excerpt with modern meaning and a sentence using each modern sense. Ac